NNAAMMEE
     aappmmdd — Advanced Power Management daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     aappmmdd [--AAaaddHHLLss] [--ff _d_e_v_n_a_m_e] [--SS _s_o_c_k_n_a_m_e] [--tt _s_e_c_o_n_d_s] [--ww _p_e_r_c_e_n_t]
          [--ZZ _p_e_r_c_e_n_t] [--zz _p_e_r_c_e_n_t]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     aappmmdd monitors the advanced power management device, apm(4), acting on
     signaled events and upon user requests as sent by the apm(8) program.

     On OpenBSD, power management is handled by the kernel, and the default
     policy can be set using the _h_w_._p_e_r_f_p_o_l_i_c_y sysctl(2).  If aappmmdd is not run‐
     ning, apm(8) is still able to query the apm(4) driver for a limited
     amount of information, but aappmmdd provides more advanced functionality,
     such as the ability to switch performance modes.

     For suspend and standby request events delivered by the BIOS, or via
     apm(8), aappmmdd runs the appropriate configuration program (if one exists),
     syncs the buffer cache to disk and initiates the requested state.  When
     resuming after suspend or standby, aappmmdd runs the appropriate configura‐
     tion program (if one exists).

     When the power status changes (external power is connected or discon‐
     nected), aappmmdd fetches the current status and reports it via syslog(3)
     with logging facility LOG_DAEMON.

     aappmmdd can change the system performance policy at startup when called with
     the --AA, --HH or --LL options, and during runtime when requested by apm(8).
     The default performance policy is "high" when connected to line current,
     and "auto" when running on battery.

     The options are as follows:

     --AA      Start aappmmdd in automatic performance adjustment mode.

     --aa      BIOS-initiated suspend or standby requests are ignored if the
             system is connected to line current and not running from batter‐
             ies (user requests are still honored).

     --dd      aappmmdd enters debug mode, staying in the foreground.  Logging out‐
             put is printed to stderr.

     --ff _d_e_v_n_a_m_e
             Specify an alternate device file name, _d_e_v_n_a_m_e.

     --HH      Start aappmmdd in manual performance adjustment mode, initialising
             _h_w_._s_e_t_p_e_r_f to 100.

     --LL      Start aappmmdd in manual performance adjustment mode, initialising
             _h_w_._s_e_t_p_e_r_f to 0.

     --SS _s_o_c_k_n_a_m_e
             Specify an alternate socket name, _s_o_c_k_n_a_m_e.  The socket is pro‐
             tected to mode 0660, UID 0, GID 0; this protects access to sus‐
             pend requests to authorized users only.

     --ss      Current battery statistics are reported via syslog(3) and aappmmdd
             exits without monitoring the APM status.

     --tt _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
             aappmmdd periodically polls the APM driver for the current power
             state.  If the battery charge level changes substantially or the
             external power status changes, the new status is logged.  The
             polling rate defaults to once per 10 minutes, but may be speci‐
             fied using the --tt command-line flag.

     --ww _p_e_r_c_e_n_t
             Log warning and enable “warnlow” actions if no AC is connected
             and the estimated battery life is equal or below _p_e_r_c_e_n_t.

     --ZZ _p_e_r_c_e_n_t
             Automatically hibernate the system if no AC is connected and the
             estimated battery life is equal or below _p_e_r_c_e_n_t.

     --zz _p_e_r_c_e_n_t
             Automatically suspend the system if no AC is connected and the
             estimated battery life is equal or below _p_e_r_c_e_n_t.

             If both --ZZ and --zz are specified, the last one will supersede the
             other.  After a resume, the effect of those options is inhibited
             for 60 seconds.

     When a client requests a suspend or stand-by state, aappmmdd does not wait
     for positive confirmation that the requested state has been entered be‐
     fore replying to the client; to do so would mean the client does not get
     a reply until the system resumes from its sleep state.  Rather, aappmmdd
     replies with the intended state to the client and then places the system
     in the requested state after running the configuration script and flush‐
     ing the buffer cache.

     Actions can be configured for the following transitions: hibernate, pow‐
     erdown, powerup, resume, standby, suspend, and warnlow.  The warnlow ac‐
     tion is run if --ww is used.  The suspend, hibernate and standby actions
     are run prior to aappmmdd performing any other actions (such as disk syncs)
     and entering the new state.  The resume program is run after resuming
     from a stand-by or suspended state.  The powerup and powerdown programs
     are run after the power status (AC connected or not) changes, as well as
     after a resume (if the power status changed in the mean time).

FFIILLEESS
     /dev/apmctl           Default device used to control apm(4).

     /etc/apm/hibernate
     /etc/apm/powerdown
     /etc/apm/powerup
     /etc/apm/resume
     /etc/apm/standby
     /etc/apm/suspend
     /etc/apm/warnlow      Action hook files which, if present, must be exe‐
                           cutable.  Each file must be an executable binary or
                           shell script.  A single program or script can be
                           used to control all transitions by examining the
                           name by which it was called.

     /etc/random.seed      Random seed file read by the bootloader; updated on
                           “hibernate”, “standby” and “suspend”.

     /var/run/apmdev       Default UNIX-domain socket used for communication
                           with apm(8).

